The threshold condition for pair production by two photons is \(E_{\gamma} \, \epsilon \ge (m_e c^2)^2\), where \(E_{\gamma}\) is the high‑energy gamma‑ray energy, \(\epsilon\) is the low‑energy target photon energy, and \(m_e c^2 = 0.511\ \text{MeV}\). Using the average CMB photon energy \(\epsilon = 10^{-3}\ \text{eV} = 10^{-9}\ \text{GeV}\):

\[
E_{\gamma}^{\text{thr}} = \frac{(0.511\ \text{MeV})^2}{\epsilon}
= \frac{(5.11\times10^{-4}\ \text{GeV})^2}{10^{-9}\ \text{GeV}}
= \frac{2.61\times10^{-7}\ \text{GeV}^2}{10^{-9}\ \text{GeV}}
= 2.61\times10^{2}\ \text{GeV}.
\]

However, this is the absolute threshold for a head‑on collision. In practice, the most effective interactions occur when the center‑of‑mass energy is a few times the threshold, and the cosmic gamma‑ray mean free path becomes comparable to the size of the observable universe for gamma‑rays of order \(10^{14}\)–\(10^{15}\ \text{eV}\) (i.e., \(10^{5}\)–\(10^{6}\ \text{GeV}\)). Using the more precise condition that the interaction becomes significant when \(E_{\gamma} \epsilon \approx 2 (m_e c^2)^2\) and accounting for the distribution of CMB photon angles, the characteristic energy is roughly

\[
E_{\gamma} \approx \frac{2 (0.511\ \text{MeV})^2}{10^{-3}\ \text{eV}}
\approx 5.2\times10^{14}\ \text{eV}
= 5.2\times10^{5}\ \text{GeV}.
\]

Among the given choices, the closest value is \(3.9\times10^{5}\ \text{GeV}\), corresponding to option (D).

